      "end_mono": 357929.900113125,
      "raw_response": "B. If the police do not inform you of your Miranda rights, they cannot interrogate you. (High confidence)\n\nANSWER: B. If the police do not inform you of your Miranda rights, they cannot interrogate you. (High confidence)\n\nAccording to the United States Supreme Court in Miranda v. Arizona, 384 U.S. 436 (1966), the prosecution cannot use statements obtained during an interrogation unless it can be proven that the detainee was aware of their right to remain silent and to an attorney during questioning, which would not be the case if they were not informed of their Miranda rights. However, the absence of a Miranda warning does not affect the legality of the arrest or the initial custody itself. The police can still conduct a custodial interview and even make an arrest if they have probable cause.\n\nReference: Miranda v. Arizona, 384 U.S. 436 (1966)",
